config TT_BH_ARC_PCIE_LTSSM_LOG
	bool "PCIe LTSSM training log"
	help
	  Record PCIe LTSSM state transitions into a CSM ring buffer that the
	  host can read back, for debugging link training failures, speed
	  downgrades and recovery storms. The buffer address is published to
	  scratch register 24 and its size to 25.

	  Blackhole is a PCIe endpoint, so on switch-attached boards the link
	  is trained by host BIOS long after PCIe init runs, and the events
	  worth seeing - link up, speed changes, recovery and EQ - keep
	  happening afterwards. Recording therefore runs on its own thread,
	  indefinitely, and pcie_init() is not delayed at all: the chip boots
	  normally and brings the link up, which is the point.

	  The thread runs at the lowest application priority and busy-polls,
	  so it consumes cycles the idle thread would otherwise take and can
	  miss transitions while descheduled. Intended for debug builds, so it
	  is off by default; build with -DCONFIG_TT_BH_ARC_PCIE_LTSSM_LOG=y to
	  enable it. Per-lane CDR lock is a separate option on top of this,
	  TT_BH_ARC_PCIE_LTSSM_LOG_CDR.

if TT_BH_ARC_PCIE_LTSSM_LOG

config TT_BH_ARC_PCIE_LTSSM_LOG_ENTRIES
	int "LTSSM log capacity in entries"
	default 4096
	range 8 32768
	help
	  Number of transitions the ring holds, shared across both PCIe
	  instances. Each entry occupies 24 bytes of CSM, so the default costs
	  96 KiB and 8192 entries cost 192 KiB. CSM is largely unused; the
	  linker will fail loudly if the region cannot fit the choice.

	  The ring retains the most recent transitions, and consecutive
	  repeats of a state - or of a two-state alternation such as Detect
	  churn - are collapsed into a repeat count rather than consuming
	  entries, so this covers far more elapsed time than its entry count
	  suggests.

config TT_BH_ARC_PCIE_LTSSM_LOG_CDR
	bool "Also log per-lane CDR lock"
	help
	  Once the link is past Detect, the recorder also reads the SerDes
	  per-lane CDR lock and logs an entry whenever it changes, so that
	  lock asserting gets its own timestamped row on the same timebase as
	  the LTSSM transitions. This uses NOC2AXI TLBs 7 and 8 in addition to
	  the log's TLB 6.

	  Off by default, because each sample costs one NOC read per lane on
	  top of the single read a pass otherwise needs, which lengthens the
	  sampling pass and so widens the window in which a short-lived LTSSM
	  state can be missed. When off, every entry records CDR as not
	  sampled (a lane count of 0), which the host readers show as "-".
	  Build with -DCONFIG_TT_BH_ARC_PCIE_LTSSM_LOG_CDR=y to enable it.

config TT_BH_ARC_PCIE_LTSSM_LOG_CDR_POLL_DIV
	int "Sample the PHY's CDR lock once every N passes"
	depends on TT_BH_ARC_PCIE_LTSSM_LOG_CDR
	default 1
	range 1 4096
	help
	  Transitions within the Recovery phases can be under a microsecond
	  apart, so on a wide link CDR resolution and LTSSM edge capture trade
	  against each other.

	  1 polls on every pass, giving the finest view of when CDR locked at
	  the largest cost to LTSSM edge capture. Raise it to protect LTSSM
	  resolution: the pass cost falls by the same factor, while the time
	  to notice a CDR change grows by it. Detect is never sampled at any
	  setting.

endif # TT_BH_ARC_PCIE_LTSSM_LOG
